{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And now forgive, I pray, my sin.</b> Since Pharaoh said, ״חָטָאתִי לַה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם וְלָכֶם״, he placed the sin against HaShem first, for it is greater, too great to bear, more than the sin he committed against Moses and Aaron. But in any case the Holy One, blessed be He, bears iniquity and multiplies forgiveness more than flesh and blood; therefore he asked him first, and afterward from HaShem, blessed be He — for he said that if Moses would forgive him first, then he would be the intercessor between him and Elohim. And in any case it would not be evil in the eyes of HaShem that he delayed in asking Him first, for the Holy One, blessed be He, is abundant in kindness and with Him, blessed be He, is forgiveness; and moreover, HaShem knows that Pharaoh needs an intercessor. Therefore it says, ״וְעַתָּה שָׂא נָא חַטָּאתִי״ — that is to say, now, immediately, ״שָׂא נָא חַטָּאתִי״, and afterward ״הַעְתִּירוּ בַּעֲדִי אֶל ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״. And as for why he said ״שָׂא נָא״ in the singular and did not say ״שְׂאוּ נָא״ in the plural — perhaps it is because Moses was raised in Pharaoh's house, and Pharaoh knew him, that he was a very humble man, forbearing and forgiving of all who slight him in the least; therefore he thought there was no need to ask forgiveness except from Aaron, whose character Pharaoh did not know." "And as for what he said, ״וְיָסֵר מֵעָלַי רַק אֶת הַמָּוֶת הַזֶּה״, some say that he asked that He remove only the live ones, for they are harmful and lethal, but not the dead ones that had already died, for they are not lethal or harmful. And the Holy One, blessed be He, did not grant his request in this and sent forth even the dead ones."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:10.997670402Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}